WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.672 --> 00:00:01.890
SPEAKER 1: Hi, Martin.

00:00:01.890 --> 00:00:03.390
What are you doing?

00:00:03.390 --> 00:00:06.690
MARTIN: I'm trying to post
a cat video to CatTube,

00:00:06.690 --> 00:00:08.039
but there is a bug.

00:00:08.039 --> 00:00:09.900
I filed an issue on
GitHub yesterday,

00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:11.730
but they haven't responded yet.

00:00:11.730 --> 00:00:13.800
I wonder if there's a
way to alert their dev

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:16.020
team when new issues are filed.

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:18.300
SPEAKER 1: Sounds like
a good job for Webhooks.

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:20.444
Stay tuned if you'd
like to know more.

00:00:20.444 --> 00:00:23.902
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:32.650
Katie is a developer at CatTube.

00:00:32.650 --> 00:00:34.420
She wants to ensure
that if someone files

00:00:34.420 --> 00:00:36.880
an issue in GitHub,
her team is notified

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:40.000
and the issue isn't
lost in her inbox.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:42.190
And honestly, we try
to read all emails,

00:00:42.190 --> 00:00:45.410
but those are just
good intentions.

00:00:45.410 --> 00:00:48.800
Luckily, GitHub is one
of the many services that

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:51.860
provides event-driven Webhooks.

00:00:51.860 --> 00:00:54.670
What this means is that
if someone files an issue,

00:00:54.670 --> 00:00:58.730
Github will send a request to a
URL specified by the developer.

00:00:58.730 --> 00:01:03.080
MARTIN: Katie can use Cloud Run
to host the new Webhook target.

00:01:03.080 --> 00:01:05.630
This means she won't have
to worry about servers,

00:01:05.630 --> 00:01:07.460
and she can use the
language and framework

00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:08.515
she's comfortable with.

00:01:08.515 --> 00:01:10.640
SPEAKER 1: The first thing
Katie has to worry about

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:12.560
is authorization.

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:16.250
A Webhook target is a public
URL, and anyone with the URL

00:01:16.250 --> 00:01:18.860
could send anything to it.

00:01:18.860 --> 00:01:21.260
To ensure she's only
processing valid requests,

00:01:21.260 --> 00:01:24.080
Katie uses a shared secret.

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:26.000
Katie creates a
secret key and puts

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:28.820
one copy in the GitHub
configuration and stores

00:01:28.820 --> 00:01:32.780
a copy securely with the
Cloud Secret Manager.

00:01:32.780 --> 00:01:34.820
Every request from
GitHub is signed,

00:01:34.820 --> 00:01:37.520
so Katie uses the secret
to validate the signature

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:40.760
and only processes
the valid requests.

00:01:40.760 --> 00:01:45.050
MARTIN: That's great, but how
does this help alert her team?

00:01:45.050 --> 00:01:48.020
SPEAKER 1: Well, now that she's
able to securely receive events

00:01:48.020 --> 00:01:51.560
from GitHub, Katie will use
these events to send messages

00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:53.000
to Slack.

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:55.760
Katie already knows how
to use the Slack API,

00:01:55.760 --> 00:01:58.730
so she writes a function to send
a message to her team's Slack

00:01:58.730 --> 00:02:01.220
channel every time a
valid request from GitHub

00:02:01.220 --> 00:02:02.390
is received.

00:02:02.390 --> 00:02:05.420
Her team will never
miss another issue.

00:02:05.420 --> 00:02:07.220
MARTIN: Katie also
wants the users

00:02:07.220 --> 00:02:10.250
to know that her team
is working on the issue.

00:02:10.250 --> 00:02:12.980
Using the same Webhook,
she writes a function

00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:16.980
to post an order response
back to the GitHub issue.

00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:19.580
SPEAKER 1: Finally, she wants
to measure how long it takes

00:02:19.580 --> 00:02:21.860
her team to close an issue.

00:02:21.860 --> 00:02:24.110
She adds one more
function to her Webhook

00:02:24.110 --> 00:02:27.140
to stream any issue events
to BigQuery so she can easily

00:02:27.140 --> 00:02:30.450
create metrics around her
team's issue performance.

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:34.250
The full Webhook target is
now creating notifications,

00:02:34.250 --> 00:02:39.050
triggering an action, and
streaming data into BigQuery.

00:02:39.050 --> 00:02:41.570
Katie was happy to solve
all three of her issues

00:02:41.570 --> 00:02:43.760
using Webhooks on Cloud Run.

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:46.070
She gave me a $1 bill.

00:02:46.070 --> 00:02:48.980
How many GitHub issues
would that process for her?

00:02:48.980 --> 00:02:51.500
MARTIN: Cloud Run pricing
is based primarily

00:02:51.500 --> 00:02:55.280
on the number of requests
and gigabyte-seconds used.

00:02:55.280 --> 00:02:57.560
Each bug filed in
GitHub will trigger

00:02:57.560 --> 00:03:01.100
one request, which will take
Cloud Run about one second

00:03:01.100 --> 00:03:02.510
to process.

00:03:02.510 --> 00:03:05.000
Each request will
stream a row of data

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:08.000
into BigQuery of
about one kilobyte.

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:11.720
Katie will get 2 million
Cloud Run requests for free

00:03:11.720 --> 00:03:15.500
but will be slightly over the
free tier on gigabyte usage.

00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:19.565
Her total Cloud Run
bill will be $0.78.

00:03:19.565 --> 00:03:23.400
For streaming and storing
the rows of one kilobyte each

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:27.270
to BigQuery, Katie
will pay $0.13.

00:03:27.270 --> 00:03:31.170
So that $1 bill would let
her process 2 million bugs in

00:03:31.170 --> 00:03:34.110
a month, with a little
change to spare.

00:03:34.110 --> 00:03:37.020
SPEAKER 1: That's a lot of bugs.

00:03:37.020 --> 00:03:38.580
MARTIN: Tune in
next time to see how

00:03:38.580 --> 00:03:42.420
Katie can set up a more robust
and fault-tolerant Webhook

00:03:42.420 --> 00:03:43.410
using Pub/Sub.

00:03:43.410 --> 00:03:46.760
[MUSIC PLAYING]

